# 5. NEW MAJOR FEATURE — ADVANCED ALARM SYSTEM INSIDE CLOCK WIDGET

This is one of the most important additions.

Do not create a separate Alarm widget.

Integrate the complete alarm manager directly into the existing Clock widget.

The Clock widget must have a manually navigable multi-view interface.

## A. Clock Widget Slides

Create two primary views.

### SLIDE 1 — Main Clock

Display:

- Current time.
- Date.
- Day of the week.
- Optional location.
- Elegant clock typography.

If the user has at least one enabled alarm, display a small alarm or bell icon on the main clock view.

The indicator must:

- Be visible without hovering.
- Be subtly positioned.
- Not interfere with the clock layout.
- Indicate that active alarms exist.
- Optionally display the number of enabled alarms.

Clicking the alarm indicator should navigate to the alarm management view.

### SLIDE 2 — Alarm Manager

Display:

- List of configured alarms.
- Time of each alarm.
- Alarm label.
- Repeat schedule.
- Enabled/disabled status.
- Ringtone indicator.
- Snooze configuration.

Provide an Add Alarm action.

Allow users to create multiple independent alarms.

## B. Manual Slide Navigation

The user must be able to switch between the main clock and alarm manager manually.

Support:

- Previous/next navigation buttons.
- Horizontal swipe gesture where supported.
- Touchpad gesture where practical.

CRITICAL:

Do not implement automatic slideshow behavior.

Do not switch views automatically after a fixed interval.

Do not rotate between views in the background.

The selected view must remain active until the user explicitly changes it.

Use subtle transitions only when the user manually navigates between views.

## C. Complete Alarm Configuration

Each alarm must support:

### Basic Information

- Alarm label.
- Alarm time.
- Enable/disable.
- One-time alarm.
- Repeating alarm.

### Repeat Schedule

Allow selecting individual weekdays:

- Monday.
- Tuesday.
- Wednesday.
- Thursday.
- Friday.
- Saturday.
- Sunday.

Provide an option to repeat every day.

Allow a one-time alarm with a specific date.

Display repeat information clearly in the alarm list.

### Ringtone Selection

Allow users to select a ringtone.

Provide:

1. Built-in available system sounds.
2. User-selected local audio files.
3. Preview button.
4. Volume configuration.
5. Stop preview button.

Use the existing Linux audio infrastructure where appropriate.

Do not download ringtones automatically.

Do not bundle unnecessary audio libraries or large sound collections.

Inspect available audio playback capabilities before selecting an implementation.

Use only audio formats supported by the selected playback backend.

Show a useful error if an unsupported audio format is selected.

### Snooze

Allow users to configure snooze duration.

Examples:

- 5 minutes.
- 10 minutes.
- 15 minutes.
- 20 minutes.
- 30 minutes.
- Custom duration.

The snooze duration should be configurable independently for each alarm.

Also provide a sensible default duration for newly created alarms.

### Alarm Actions

When an alarm triggers, provide:

- Stop.
- Snooze.
- Display alarm label.
- Display scheduled time.

The alarm notification should remain available until acknowledged.

Do not silently dismiss alarms.

## D. Multiple Alarms

Users must be able to create and store many alarms.

Do not limit the system to a single active alarm.

Alarms must be displayed in a vertically arranged list.

For example:

07:00 — Wake Up  
08:00 — Morning Study  
12:30 — Lunch Reminder  
18:00 — Evening Activity  
21:00 — Prepare for Tomorrow

Allow:

- Edit.
- Delete.
- Enable.
- Disable.
- Reorder where appropriate.

If multiple alarms become due at the same time, do not overwrite or discard them.

Preserve every due alarm and present them together in an active alarm queue or grouped notification.

Avoid playing multiple ringtone streams simultaneously.

Use a controlled playback and notification queue.

## E. Alarm Persistence and Reliability

The alarm system must work independently of whether the Clock widget is currently visible.

The alarm configuration must persist across:

- Widget reload.
- Plasma restart.
- Application restart.
- User logout and subsequent login.

Store alarm data in the existing local database.

The Rust backend should be responsible for alarm scheduling.

Do not implement alarm scheduling exclusively with a QML Timer.

Use the existing shared Rust service.

The scheduler should:

- Calculate the next upcoming alarm.
- Sleep until the next relevant event.
- Recalculate when alarms change.
- Recalculate when the system clock changes.
- Handle timezone changes.
- Re-evaluate due alarms after suspend/resume.
- Avoid frequent background polling.
- Avoid spawning unnecessary threads or processes.

Use the existing session startup mechanism or a lightweight user-level service where appropriate.

Do not create another unnecessary background daemon if the existing backend can perform this task.

Document that alarms require an active user session and cannot ring while the computer is fully powered off.

## F. Alarm Notifications

Integrate with the existing Linux desktop notification capabilities.

When an alarm triggers:

- Show a clear alarm notification.
- Include Stop and Snooze actions where supported.
- Play the selected ringtone.
- Update the alarm state.

Ensure simultaneous alarms are not lost.

Make the active alarm state recoverable after application reload where practical.

