# 8. GLOBAL FEATURE — CUSTOM IMAGE BACKGROUND FOR EVERY WIDGET

This is another major feature.

All widgets must support individual background customization using local image files.

This must be implemented as a reusable global widget background system.

It must not be separately implemented with inconsistent logic in every widget.

## A. Supported Formats

Support:

- SVG.
- JPG.
- JPEG.
- PNG.
- WebP.

The system must verify actual format decoding support in the target Qt 5 installation.

If an optional image plugin or Qt module is required, inspect availability before adding dependencies.

Do not claim support for a format unless the application can successfully display it.

Do not silently display broken images.

Show a useful error when a file cannot be decoded.

## B. Widget Background Configuration

Every widget must support an individual background image:

- Clock.
- Clock Alarm View.
- Calendar.
- Weather.
- Pomodoro.
- Agenda.
- Photo Frame.
- Quick Actions.
- System Monitor.
- Notes.
- Todo List.
- Any future widgets.

Users must be able to select a different image for each widget.

Widget backgrounds must not automatically change other widgets.

## C. Background Controls

Provide:

- Select image.
- Change image.
- Remove image.
- Reset to default.
- Preview image.
- Image fit mode.
- Image position.
- Surface overlay opacity.
- Optional color tint.

Image fit modes:

1. Cover.
2. Contain.
3. Stretch.
4. Center.

Default should be Cover for photographic widget backgrounds.

## D. Readability Controls

When an image is used as a background, preserve readable text.

Provide:

- Adjustable dark overlay.
- Adjustable light overlay.
- Overlay opacity.
- Automatic contrast option where practical.
- Manual text color override.

The user should be able to choose whether the content uses light or dark typography.

Do not allow text to disappear against bright or dark images.

## E. Background Architecture

Create one reusable background component or abstraction.

Suggested component:

WidgetBackground.qml

It should handle:

- Image loading.
- Format validation.
- Image cropping.
- Image positioning.
- Opacity.
- Surface tint.
- Rounded clipping.
- Fallback behavior.

All widgets should consume this shared component.

Do not duplicate image-processing code in every widget.

## F. Performance Requirements

Custom backgrounds must not make URWidgets significantly heavier.

Implement:

- Lazy image loading.
- Source-size-aware decoding.
- Thumbnail caching where appropriate.
- Image resource reuse.
- Proper resource release.
- No repeated decoding when the widget is merely redrawn.

Do not load original full-resolution images unnecessarily when the widget is small.

Do not constantly process SVG images.

Cache rendered assets where appropriate.

Avoid expensive blur filters on large photographs.

The image should be loaded once and reused while its configuration remains unchanged.

## G. Local File Handling

Use an appropriate native file picker.

Persist selected local image paths in the existing configuration system.

Handle missing or moved files gracefully.

If the original file is unavailable, show the default widget surface and provide an option to select another image.

Do not automatically copy large image files into the application's database.

