# 10. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE AND RESOURCE EFFICIENCY

Performance must remain a top priority.

URWidgets should not become significantly heavier just because a few modules are added.

## A. Shared Rust Backend

Reuse the current Rust backend.

Extend its existing modules for:

- Alarm scheduling.
- Notes persistence.
- Todo persistence.
- Background configuration management.
- Existing widget data.

Do not create separate long-running backend processes for individual features.

## B. Tauri

If Tauri is already used for the settings application, keep it.

Do not launch additional Tauri WebViews for every desktop widget.

Avoid using Tauri for tasks already handled efficiently by the current native desktop widget interface.

Do not increase process count unnecessarily.

## C. IPC

Reuse the existing IPC implementation.

Do not introduce additional communication mechanisms unless technically necessary.

Communication should remain asynchronous where appropriate.

Avoid blocking widget rendering while waiting for backend operations.

## D. Database

Use the current SQLite database.

Implement safe database migrations.

Suggested additional tables:

- alarms
- notes
- todo_items
- widget_backgrounds

Adapt these to the existing schema if equivalent structures already exist.

Do not duplicate data structures.

Use reasonable indexes and transactions.

## E. Scheduling

Do not create one permanent polling loop per feature.

Prefer a shared scheduling mechanism.

The alarm scheduler should sleep until its next event.

Notes and Todo should update only when the user modifies data.

Image backgrounds should not be reprocessed on every frame.

## F. Rendering

Keep the widget UI lightweight.

Avoid:

- Excessive QML nesting.
- Expensive ShaderEffects.
- Continuously animated gradients.
- Unnecessary blur.
- Frequent image decoding.
- Unnecessary full-widget repainting.
- Repeated IPC polling.
- Duplicate timers.

Use simple, reusable components.

## G. Dependency Management

Before introducing any new dependency:

1. Check whether the project already has a suitable library.
2. Check whether the dependency is compatible with the existing versions.
3. Explain why it is necessary.
4. Prefer small, maintained dependencies.
5. Avoid pulling in large frameworks for small features.

